export const LESSONS = [
  // Module 1 — Shared PPC Foundation
  lesson('cppc-m1-l1', 'cppc-m1', 1, 'What Centralized PPC Is Responsible For',
    'Across every platform in the source material, Centralized PPC is described as the team that owns paid-advertising operations end to end — not just building a campaign, but planning it, launching it, watching it, adjusting it, and reporting on it. The Amazon handbook frames this as a full lifecycle running from product eligibility checks through to a 21-day margin review. The Google Ads sources frame the same idea around protecting and scaling what already works, rather than only launching new campaigns. The eBay and Meta sources add campaign-proposal discipline: a new campaign idea is written up, justified, and checked before it goes live. This programme teaches these platform-specific shapes as expressions of one underlying job — running paid advertising responsibly, with evidence, across whichever platform is in scope. A mistake to avoid: assuming Centralized PPC\'s job ends once a campaign is switched on — every source in this programme treats "launch" as roughly the midpoint of the work, not the end of it.',
    5, 'Amazon PPC Campaign Management BGCT Standard Handbook, Overview section; eBay Ads BGCT, Creator/purpose line'),
  lesson('cppc-m1-l2', 'cppc-m1', 2, 'Recommendation Versus Approval',
    'A theme repeats across every platform this programme covers: a tool, a rule engine, or a piece of analysis can produce a recommendation, but the sources never treat that recommendation as the same thing as an approved decision. The Meta proposal in this programme is the clearest example — its own header states plainly that manager approval is required before any campaign can launch, no matter how well-reasoned the proposal is. Amazon\'s rule engines describe automated conditions and actions, but pair them with named roles that can create, edit, or disable a rule, implying human oversight of the rule itself. The Google Ads sources go further, describing specific human-approval gates (for example, before a large budget increase) that sit on top of any automated monitoring. This programme treats "a recommendation exists" and "a decision has been approved" as two different states, always. A mistake to avoid: treating a system-generated flag, score, or suggestion as if it were already an approved action — nothing in this programme\'s sources supports that shortcut.',
    5, 'FB Ads New Campaign Proposal, header approval requirement; What We Lost and Why, budget-approval rule'),
  lesson('cppc-m1-l3', 'cppc-m1', 3, 'Evidence and Documentation Discipline',
    'Every platform in this programme shares the same underlying expectation: a decision, a change, or an instruction that is not written down somewhere the team can see is treated as if it did not happen. The Amazon handbook states this most directly — if the knowledge is not written in the handbook itself, it "does not exist as company knowledge," and verbal instruction alone is not enough. The Google Ads incident-review source describes a real cost that came from exactly this gap: a strong campaign was not protected because no one had documented that it needed protecting, and the resulting rules explicitly require handover documentation before an operator\'s work is lost. The eBay sources describe a comparable weekly reporting duty. This programme treats "record it" as a habit that applies everywhere, not a rule specific to one platform. A mistake to avoid: assuming a decision explained in conversation or chat is equivalent to a decision recorded in the team\'s shared record — the sources consistently treat only the second as real.',
    4, 'Amazon PPC Campaign Management BGCT Standard Handbook, "No-Rule Point"; What We Lost and Why, handover rule'),
  lesson('cppc-m1-l4', 'cppc-m1', 4, 'Confidentiality Boundary for This Programme',
    'The Centralized PPC source documents this programme is built from contain real account names, real campaign identifiers, real historical spend and sales figures, and named individual staff members in several places. None of that appears anywhere in this programme. Every example used in a lesson, a Skill Check question, or the final practical task is fictional and neutral — a made-up product, a made-up account, a made-up figure — chosen only to illustrate a concept the sources actually teach. Where a source names a person in a governance role (someone who owns a document, approves a budget change, or leads a review), this programme uses the role title only, such as "Amazon Rule Owner" or "Google Ads Team Lead," never the individual\'s name. This is not a simplification for convenience; it is a hard boundary that applies to every module in this programme. A mistake to avoid: assuming a "for example" in a lesson or question reflects a real account or a real result — it never does here.',
    3, 'Programme-wide confidentiality rule — see Programme Sources'),

  // Module 2 — Amazon Campaign Foundations & Eligibility
  lesson('cppc-m2-l1', 'cppc-m2', 1, 'Eligibility Gates Before Any Campaign Work Begins',
    'The Amazon handbook describes advertising work as gated behind two checks that must both be satisfied before a campaign is even planned: a product-eligibility check and a listing-quality check. The product check looks at whether the product itself is a sound candidate for paid traffic — enough stock, a workable price point, and a track record that will not embarrass the brand once ads start sending it clicks. The listing check looks at whether the product page itself is ready to convert that traffic — clear images, a complete title, and the supporting content a shopper needs to decide to buy. The handbook is explicit that both gates must be passed, not just one; a well-stocked product with a thin listing is treated the same as a well-written listing for a product that is not ready to sell in volume. A mistake to avoid: starting keyword research or campaign build work before both gates have been confirmed — the source treats this as work done in the wrong order, not simply optional prep.',
    5, 'Amazon PPC Campaign Management BGCT Standard Handbook, Gate 1 (Product Eligibility) and Gate 2 (Listing Quality) sections'),
  lesson('cppc-m2-l2', 'cppc-m2', 2, 'Amazon Campaign Types and How They Target',
    'The sources describe three Amazon campaign types Centralized PPC works with: Sponsored Products, which places ads for individual products directly in search results and on product pages; Sponsored Brands, which promotes a brand or a small group of products together, often at the top of a search results page; and Sponsored Display, which can target based on product context or on audiences built from past shopper behaviour, such as people who viewed a similar product recently. Within Sponsored Products specifically, targeting can be automatic — letting Amazon match the ad to relevant searches — or manual, where the team chooses specific keywords or competing products to target directly. This structure matters because later modules in this Amazon track (bid logic, budget logic, pause and reactivation logic) are all described in terms of which campaign type and targeting method they apply to. A mistake to avoid: treating "Amazon advertising" as one undifferentiated thing — the rule logic taught in the next several modules genuinely differs by campaign type.',
    5, 'What is the Amazon Campaign? Guidebook, campaign-type and targeting sections'),
  lesson('cppc-m2-l3', 'cppc-m2', 3, 'A Fixed Naming Convention Across Every Campaign',
    'Amazon campaigns in this programme follow one consistent naming shape: the ad type, the product or product group, the targeting method, and — where relevant — the match type or audience. A Sponsored Products automatic campaign for a given product, for example, is named differently from a Sponsored Products manual keyword campaign for that same product, even though both promote the same item, because the targeting method is part of the name. The purpose of this convention is entirely practical: anyone on the team should be able to tell what a campaign does and how it targets just by reading its name, without opening it. This is a small rule with a large effect — every later lesson in this Amazon track assumes campaigns are named this way, so that a rule engine or a report referring to "the Sponsored Products manual campaign" for a product is referring to exactly one, unambiguous thing. A mistake to avoid: naming a campaign after only the product, and leaving the targeting method to be inferred from memory — the convention exists specifically so that inference is never required.',
    4, 'Amazon PPC Campaign Management BGCT Standard Handbook, naming convention section'),
  lesson('cppc-m2-l4', 'cppc-m2', 4, 'Negative Keywords and the Default Bidding Baseline',
    'Two baseline settings apply to a new Amazon campaign before it has any performance data of its own. First, a fixed starting list of negative keywords — generic, low-intent terms unlikely to lead to a sale, such as searches for free items, secondhand goods, or how-to content — is applied so early spend is not wasted on traffic that was never going to convert. Second, new campaigns start on a cautious bidding strategy that can only reduce Amazon\'s suggested bid, not increase it automatically; a more flexible strategy that can also raise bids is only introduced later, once a campaign has proven it can hold a healthy ACOS over a sustained period. Both defaults exist for the same reason: a brand-new campaign has no track record yet, so the safest assumption is used until it earns a more flexible one. A mistake to avoid: switching a new campaign straight onto the more flexible bidding strategy "to save time" — the sources describe this flexibility as something a campaign proves it deserves, not a starting default.',
    4, 'Amazon PPC Campaign Management BGCT Standard Handbook, negative-keyword baseline and bidding-strategy sections'),

  // Module 3 — Amazon Bid & Placement Optimization Logic
  lesson('cppc-m3-l1', 'cppc-m3', 1, 'A Two-Tier Evaluation: Longer Window Sets Direction, Shorter Window Fine-Tunes',
    'The Amazon bid rule engine described in the developer specification evaluates a campaign or keyword using two time windows at once, and the two windows do different jobs. A longer performance window (the sources use 30 days) sets the general performance category a campaign falls into — broadly, how efficiently it has been converting ad spend into sales over a meaningful stretch of time. A shorter window (7 days) is then used inside that category to fine-tune the bid up or down, reacting to more recent, more volatile performance without letting a single bad or good week override the longer-term picture entirely. This two-tier shape — a stable outer classification and a responsive inner adjustment — is the same underlying pattern used again in the budget engine covered in the next module. A mistake to avoid: reacting only to the most recent few days of performance data when deciding a bid change — the sources always classify first using the longer window, and only fine-tune using the shorter one.',
    5, 'Bid Optimization Rule Configurator, Overview & Purpose and campaign-type structure sections'),
  lesson('cppc-m3-l2', 'cppc-m3', 2, 'ACOS-Based Bid Direction, Illustrated',
    'Inside the two-tier structure from the previous lesson, the direction of a bid change follows a consistent logic: strong recent efficiency (a low ACOS alongside real order volume) tends to justify a small bid increase, aiming to win more of that already-profitable traffic; poor recent efficiency (spend without matching sales, or a high ACOS) tends to justify a small bid decrease, aiming to protect budget from continuing to fund traffic that is not converting. A campaign that is spending very little and generating almost no data yet is treated differently again — the engine nudges its bid up slightly, on the logic that it needs a bit more visibility before its performance can be judged fairly at all. As a fictional illustration only (not a source-stated universal threshold): a keyword with strong recent orders and a low ACOS might see its bid nudged up a few percentage points, while one with meaningful spend and zero recent orders might see its bid nudged down by a similar margin. A mistake to avoid: assuming every underperforming keyword should be cut aggressively straight away — the engine\'s adjustments are described as small, incremental nudges, not sudden large swings.',
    5, 'Bid Optimization Rule Configurator, bid-adjustment condition tables (illustrative interpretation, not a reproduced table)'),
  lesson('cppc-m3-l3', 'cppc-m3', 3, 'Placement-Level Bid Adjustments',
    'Beyond keyword- or product-level bids, Amazon also allows a separate bid adjustment for where an ad appears — commonly described in the sources as the top of search results, the rest of the search results, and product detail pages. The same longer-window-then-shorter-window logic from the previous two lessons applies here too, but the adjustment is expressed as a percentage change to the bid for that specific placement rather than a flat amount. The underlying idea is that the same keyword or product can perform differently depending on where its ad shows up, so treating every placement identically can waste the efficiency gains available from favouring the placements that convert best for a given campaign. A mistake to avoid: treating placement adjustments as a one-time setup step — the sources describe them being reviewed on the same recurring cadence as bids themselves, not configured once and left alone.',
    4, 'Bid Optimization Rule Configurator, placement bid-adjustment sections (Top of Search, Rest of Search, Product Pages)'),
  lesson('cppc-m3-l4', 'cppc-m3', 4, 'Roles, Permissions, and the Audit-Log Requirement',
    'The Amazon rule configurators consistently describe three access levels for anyone working with the bid and budget rule engines: a Lead-level role with full ability to view, create, edit, and disable rules; a Team Leader-level role that can view, create, and edit rules but cannot delete or disable them; and a Staff-level role limited to viewing rules in read-only form. Alongside this permission structure, the specifications require an audit log recording who changed a rule, when, and what exactly was changed. Together, these two requirements describe a system where rule changes are traceable to a specific person and a specific moment, not anonymous or reconstructed after the fact from memory. A mistake to avoid: treating the audit-log requirement as a "nice to have" reporting feature — the sources present it as a core requirement of the rule-configurator system itself, on the same footing as the rule logic it is logging.',
    4, 'Bid Optimization Rule Configurator, User Access Control section'),

  // Module 4 — Amazon Budget Optimization & Special Rules
  lesson('cppc-m4-l1', 'cppc-m4', 1, 'Choosing Between a Longer-Window and a Month-to-Date ACOS Figure',
    'Amazon\'s budget rule engine needs an ACOS figure to work from each day, and the sources describe switching which figure is used depending roughly on where in the calendar month today falls: earlier in the month, a longer trailing window (the sources use 30 days) is used because it is more statistically stable; later in the month, a month-to-date figure is used instead, because it reflects more recent, more relevant performance once enough days of the current month have accumulated to be meaningful on their own. The exact day of the month where this switch happens is not consistent across the sibling Amazon UK source documents that describe it, so this programme does not teach a specific cut-off date — only the underlying concept that the engine deliberately trades stability for recency as the month goes on. A mistake to avoid: assuming a single one-size-fits-all switch date applies everywhere in Amazon\'s rule set — the sources themselves are not fully consistent on this exact point, which is exactly why this programme treats the date itself as excluded.',
    5, 'Budget Optimization Rule Configurator, date-window/ACOS-source-selection sections (exact switch date excluded — see Programme Sources)'),
  lesson('cppc-m4-l2', 'cppc-m4', 2, 'Grouping Campaigns by Performance for a Budget Response',
    'Once an ACOS figure has been chosen for the day, the budget engine groups a campaign into a performance category and gives it a matching budget response — stronger, more efficient performance earns a larger budget increase, while weaker performance earns a smaller one or none at all. Precisely how many categories exist and where their boundaries sit differs between sibling Amazon UK rule documents describing what should be the same rule family, so this programme teaches only the shared underlying shape — categorise by recent ACOS efficiency, then respond with a budget change sized to that category — without asserting a specific number of categories or a specific boundary value as the confirmed, universal Amazon rule. A mistake to avoid: assuming every Amazon campaign type uses an identical number of performance tiers — the source documents describing different campaign types are not consistent with each other on this exact structural point.',
    5, 'Budget Optimization Rule Configurator, category-mapping sections (exact tier count/boundaries excluded — see Programme Sources)'),
  lesson('cppc-m4-l3', 'cppc-m4', 3, 'The Hourly Intraday Budget-Boost Concept',
    'Separate from the once-a-day budget review, the sources describe an hourly check that can top up a campaign\'s daily budget mid-day if it is running efficiently and is close to exhausting what it was allocated that morning. The underlying logic is that a strong campaign spending through its budget quickly is a signal of demand, not a problem, and a small hourly top-up lets it keep capturing that demand instead of going dark for the rest of the day once the original budget runs out. This concept is applied only to campaigns performing well by the ACOS standard described in the earlier daily review, and only up to a defined ceiling relative to the campaign\'s low starting budget — it is a targeted top-up for a specific situation, not a general hourly budget increase applied everywhere. A mistake to avoid: assuming the hourly boost applies to every campaign — the sources gate it specifically to campaigns that are both efficient and close to running out of their allocated budget for the day.',
    4, 'Hour-Basic Budget Optimization Rule Configurator, hourly boost condition and constraint sections'),
  lesson('cppc-m4-l4', 'cppc-m4', 4, 'Order-Volume Overrides That Take Priority',
    'Beyond the standard category-based budget response, the sources describe a set of special override rules that apply when a campaign has both a consistently low ACOS across the longer and shorter windows and a meaningfully high order volume. When an override condition is met, it sets the campaign\'s budget directly rather than nudging it incrementally, and it is explicitly described as taking priority over the standard category-based calculation covered earlier in this module — in effect, "this campaign has proven itself enough that the normal step-by-step budget logic no longer applies; give it what it needs." This programme teaches the concept and its priority ordering without stating a specific order-volume threshold, since the exact number is one of several values that varies across the sibling rule documents this concept was drawn from. A mistake to avoid: applying both the standard category rule and an override rule together — the sources describe the override as replacing the standard calculation for that campaign, not adding to it.',
    4, 'Budget Optimization Rule Configurator, special/order-volume override sections'),

  // Module 5 — Amazon Product Activation & Pause Automation
  lesson('cppc-m5-l1', 'cppc-m5', 1, 'Reactivating a Short-Paused Product',
    'When a product\'s advertising has been paused for a relatively short time, the sources describe a "first match wins" evaluation: a list of conditions is checked in order, each built from a mix of ROAS across a longer and a shorter window, recent order count, and recent ad spend as a share of the product\'s price, and the product is reactivated as soon as any one condition in the list is satisfied. This shape favours speed for a strong, low-risk product — the very first condition in the list is usually the easiest one to satisfy for a genuinely strong performer, so the strongest candidates are reactivated with minimal delay, while a marginal product may need to satisfy a more specific, price-tiered condition further down the list. A mistake to avoid: assuming every condition on the list needs to be checked and combined together — the sources describe reactivation triggering as soon as the first matching condition is found, not after every condition has been evaluated.',
    5, 'Product Re-Activation Rule Configurator, Rule 1 — short-paused ASINs section'),
  lesson('cppc-m5-l2', 'cppc-m5', 2, 'Reactivating a Long-Paused Product',
    'For a product that has been paused for a longer stretch of time, the same first-match-wins logic from the previous lesson still applies, but the evaluation windows are extended (the sources use 60-day and 30-day windows in place of the 30-day and 7-day windows used for a short pause), and the rule set only runs at all once the short-pause rule set has already been checked and found no match. The longer window makes sense given the longer pause: a product that has been quiet for a while needs a longer, more reliable performance picture before being switched back on, rather than reacting to a short recent blip. A mistake to avoid: applying the long-pause rule set to every paused product regardless of how long it has actually been paused — the sources describe it specifically as the fallback tier, used only once the short-pause rule set has already been tried.',
    4, 'Product Re-Activation Rule Configurator, Rule 2 — long-paused ASINs section'),
  lesson('cppc-m5-l3', 'cppc-m5', 3, 'Spend- and ROAS-Based Pause Logic',
    'Pausing a product follows a mirror-image logic to reactivation: a product with real order history in both the longer and shorter windows is evaluated primarily on ROAS trends, while a product with orders in the longer window but none recently is evaluated on a combination of ROAS and how much of its own price has been spent chasing a sale that has not come; a product with no orders in either window at all is evaluated on spend and click volume alone. In every case, the sources also describe a "do not pause" exception: if the shorter, more recent window shows a genuinely improving trend even though the longer window still looks weak, the pause is held off rather than triggered on stale data. This mirrors the two-tier direction logic already introduced for bids and budgets in earlier modules — a longer window sets the general picture, and a shorter window is checked before acting on it. A mistake to avoid: pausing a product based only on its longer-window figures without checking whether the shorter window shows it is already recovering.',
    5, 'Spend-Basic Product Pause Rule Configurator, ROAS-based and spend-based pause sections'),
  lesson('cppc-m5-l4', 'cppc-m5', 4, 'Price-Tiered Spend Thresholds and Stock-Based Pausing',
    'Where a pause condition depends on how much has been spent relative to a product\'s price, the sources consistently use a price-tiered table: cheaper products are allowed to spend a larger share of their own price before triggering a pause, while more expensive products are held to a smaller share, on the logic that a fixed percentage of a high price represents a much larger absolute risk than the same percentage of a low price. Separately, a distinct stock-based pause concept exists for products the sources describe as "fast-moving" — advertising is paused automatically once available stock falls below a defined level, specifically to avoid running ads (and paying for clicks) for a product that is about to run out and cannot fulfil the resulting demand. This programme teaches the shape of both ideas without stating the exact qualifying conditions for "fast-moving," since the sibling source documents that define it do not fully agree with each other. A mistake to avoid: assuming a stock-based pause and a spend-based pause are the same mechanism — the sources treat them as two separate, independently triggered rule families.',
    4, 'Spend-Basic Product Pause Rule Configurator, price-tier tables; User-Level Product Pause Workflow (Stock-Based) — Amazon UK (fast-moving definition excluded — see Programme Sources)'),

  // Module 6 — Amazon TACOS Monitoring & Escalation
  lesson('cppc-m6-l1', 'cppc-m6', 1, 'What TACOS Measures and Why It Complements ACOS',
    'TACOS (Total Advertising Cost of Sale) is calculated as total ad spend divided by total revenue — but unlike ACOS, "total revenue" here means both ad-driven sales and organic (non-ad) sales added together. The sources are explicit about why this second figure matters alongside ACOS: a product can carry a high ACOS on its ad campaigns specifically while still being genuinely healthy overall, if its advertising is helping drive organic sales growth on top of the ad-attributed sales; conversely, a product with an acceptable-looking ACOS but flat or falling organic sales may be masking a weaker underlying picture. The sources also stress that TACOS should be measured at the individual product level, not only at the campaign level, because campaign-level figures can average away a genuinely underperforming product hidden among stronger ones. A mistake to avoid: treating a low ACOS alone as proof a product is healthy — the sources treat TACOS as the check that catches what ACOS on its own can miss.',
    5, 'User-Level TACOS Workflow — Amazon UK, Phase 1 (Definition & Data Sourcing) and Phase 2 (Product-Level Calculation) sections'),
  lesson('cppc-m6-l2', 'cppc-m6', 2, 'Product-Lifecycle Target Bands',
    'The sources describe a different target TACOS depending on how established a product is, measured by its order count: a newly launched product with relatively few orders is given a wider, more forgiving target range, on the logic that it needs advertising support to build initial momentum; a growing product with a moderate order history is given a tighter target; and a mature product with a substantial order history is expected to reach the tightest target of all, since it should by then be able to rely more on organic demand and less on ad spend to keep selling. This lifecycle-aware approach avoids judging every product against the same fixed bar regardless of how long it has been on sale. A mistake to avoid: comparing a newly launched product\'s TACOS directly against a mature product\'s target — the sources deliberately set a different bar for each lifecycle stage.',
    4, 'User-Level TACOS Workflow — Amazon UK, Phase 3 (Supplementary Metrics & Target-Setting) section'),
  lesson('cppc-m6-l3', 'cppc-m6', 3, 'The TACOS Gap and What It Triggers',
    'The sources define the TACOS Gap as the difference between a product\'s actual TACOS and its lifecycle target from the previous lesson, and use that gap to decide what action, if any, is needed: a large positive gap (actual TACOS well above target) signals meaningful overspend relative to what the product is generating and points toward pausing or cutting bids; a smaller positive gap points toward a lighter optimisation pass rather than a hard stop; a gap close to zero or slightly negative is treated as healthy and left alone; and a clearly negative gap — spending noticeably less than the target would allow — is treated as an opportunity to scale up, not just a pass. This turns a single comparison (actual versus target) into a specific recommended direction, rather than leaving "is this good or bad" as an open question. A mistake to avoid: treating every TACOS figure above target as equally urgent — the sources scale the response to the size of the gap, not just its direction.',
    4, 'User-Level TACOS Workflow — Amazon UK, Phase 3 (TACOS Gap) section'),
  lesson('cppc-m6-l4', 'cppc-m6', 4, 'From Raw Metrics to an Escalation Layer',
    'Once TACOS, its gap, and an auto-generated flag (for example, a pause recommendation, a bid-reduction recommendation, or a scale-up recommendation) have been calculated for a product, the sources describe that information flowing from a raw metrics dashboard into a separate decision layer referred to only as the "PH Dashboard" in the source material, which records who owns the resulting action, when it was actioned, and when the result should be checked. This programme flags one open point plainly: the sources never explain what "PH" stands for or confirm any relationship between this escalation layer and the unrelated PH Team onboarding programme that already exists in this platform — this programme does not assume any connection between the two, and neither should a learner. A mistake to avoid: assuming an auto-generated flag has already been actioned once it appears on the raw metrics dashboard — the sources describe a separate decision layer, with its own named owner and action date, sitting between the flag and the actual response.',
    3, 'User-Level TACOS Workflow — Amazon UK, Phase 4 (Auto-Flagging & Escalation) section — "PH Dashboard" relationship to any other programme is unconfirmed; see Programme Sources'),

  // Module 7 — Amazon Multi-Market Operations & Scaling
  lesson('cppc-m7-l1', 'cppc-m7', 1, 'A Reference Template, Not a Fixed Multi-Market Policy',
    'Several of the Amazon rule configurators state directly that their documented rules were built specifically around the UK account, and are meant to be used as a reference when building an equivalent rule set for another market — not applied unmodified everywhere. This is an important distinction: everything taught in Modules 3 through 6 of this Amazon track describes the UK-based rule shape and, where non-conflicting, UK-specific illustrative values; it should not be assumed to transfer automatically, unchanged, to a different marketplace. The sources are consistent that the underlying rule shape (two-tier evaluation windows, category-based responses, price-tiered thresholds, and so on) is the reusable part, while the specific numeric thresholds are explicitly called out as needing local adaptation. A mistake to avoid: assuming a UK-specific figure taught earlier in this Amazon track applies unchanged to another market — the sources themselves describe it as a starting reference, not a finished multi-market rule.',
    5, 'Budget Optimization Rule Configurator; Bid Optimization Rule Configurator — Scope statements'),
  lesson('cppc-m7-l2', 'cppc-m7', 2, 'What Changes Between Markets, and What Should Not',
    'The rule shapes taught throughout this Amazon track — evaluation windows, category-based responses, price-tiered spend logic, escalation via TACOS Gap — are described as portable across markets; what is expected to change is the currency the thresholds are expressed in and the specific numeric values themselves, since a given ACOS or spend threshold appropriate for one market\'s pricing and competitive landscape will not automatically be right for another. During discovery, several Amazon DE, FR & IT source documents were found still expressing their thresholds in the UK\'s currency rather than a currency appropriate to those markets — an unresolved localisation gap this programme does not repair or silently correct. A mistake to avoid: assuming every number that appears in a DE, FR & IT source document has already been correctly adapted for that market — discovery found clear evidence that some of it has not been.',
    4, 'User-Level Daily Budget and Hour-Basic Optimization Workflows — Amazon DE, FR & IT — currency-localisation gap noted, not corrected; see Programme Sources'),
  lesson('cppc-m7-l3', 'cppc-m7', 3, 'A Draft Status, Not Yet a Single Source of Truth',
    'Beyond the currency issue in the previous lesson, several of the Amazon DE, FR & IT rule documents found during discovery contain two separate, named-author versions of essentially the same rule sitting side by side, with different threshold values and even different daily execution times, and no indication that the two versions have been reconciled into one agreed rule. This is a meaningfully different situation from the UK rule documents, which each present a single, apparently-settled rule set. This programme treats the DE, FR & IT material as a genuine draft-in-progress rather than a finished, ready-to-teach policy, and does not present any of its specific unreconciled values as confirmed. A mistake to avoid: treating a DE, FR & IT rule document as equally authoritative to its UK counterpart — the sources themselves show clear signs the DE, FR & IT material has not reached the same level of agreement yet.',
    4, 'User-Level Daily Budget and Hour-Basic Optimization Workflows — Amazon DE, FR & IT — dual-author draft status noted; see Programme Sources'),
  lesson('cppc-m7-l4', 'cppc-m7', 4, 'Margin Review and Scaling',
    'Closing out the Amazon track, the handbook describes a recurring margin review — checking that a product\'s advertising is not eroding its profitability faster than its sales are building it — at a fixed point after launch, alongside a general principle for scaling a proven campaign: increases to budget or bid are made in modest, incremental steps once a campaign has demonstrated sustained strong performance, rather than in one large jump. This mirrors the same cautious, evidence-first pattern already seen throughout this Amazon track — a new or recovering campaign earns more flexibility gradually, based on a track record, rather than being given it up front. A mistake to avoid: scaling a campaign\'s budget or bid sharply the moment it shows one strong day or week — the sources consistently favour incremental scaling built on a sustained pattern, not a single good result.',
    3, 'Amazon PPC Campaign Management BGCT Standard Handbook, margin-review and scaling sections'),

  // Module 8 — Google Ads Strategy, Kill Gates & Campaign Governance
  lesson('cppc-m8-l1', 'cppc-m8', 1, 'Kill Gates: Automatically Pausing a Clear Underperformer',
    'The Google Ads sources describe a small set of "kill gates" — spend-and-outcome combinations severe enough that a campaign is paused automatically rather than left to a routine review cycle. The underlying shapes are: spending a defined amount with zero conversions, which triggers a pause within a set time; spending a larger defined amount while ROAS stays below breakeven, which triggers both a pause and an escalation; and accumulating a meaningful number of clicks with a conversion rate low enough to suggest the problem is the product listing rather than the bidding, which also triggers a pause. These gates exist specifically to stop clearly bad spend quickly, without waiting for a scheduled weekly or monthly review to catch it. A mistake to avoid: treating a kill gate as equivalent to a soft warning that can be reviewed "when there\'s time" — the sources describe these as automatic pause triggers, not optional flags.',
    5, 'Google PPC Strategy — 17 Months of Live Data, kill-gate sections; What We Lost and Why, Rules K-1 through K-3'),
  lesson('cppc-m8-l2', 'cppc-m8', 2, '"Protected" Campaigns and the Budget-Change Ceiling',
    'On the opposite end from a kill gate, the sources describe a "protected" status for a campaign that has shown a strong return in a given month: once protected, that campaign requires an approver\'s explicit sign-off before its structure, targeting, or budget can be changed, specifically to prevent a well-meaning but poorly timed adjustment from disrupting something that is already working. A separate, related rule caps any single budget increase at a defined percentage without written approval, regardless of whether the campaign is formally protected or not — the sources describe this ceiling as a guardrail against reacting too aggressively to a single good result. Both rules share the same underlying caution already seen in the Amazon track: strong performance is protected and scaled carefully, not treated as an invitation to make large, fast changes. A mistake to avoid: assuming a strong campaign no longer needs any oversight — the sources describe strong performance as the specific trigger for extra protection, not less.',
    5, 'What We Lost and Why, Rules P-1, P-2, and H-4'),
  lesson('cppc-m8-l3', 'cppc-m8', 3, 'Handover: Protecting a Campaign When Its Operator Changes',
    'The Google Ads incident-review source describes a real cost that followed from a strong campaign losing its context when the person managing it moved on, with no documented record of why it was working or what needed to be protected. The resulting rule requires a specific handover document before an operator\'s work is treated as closed out — covering what the campaign is doing, why it is working, and what must not be changed carelessly by whoever picks it up next. This connects directly back to the evidence-and-documentation theme from Module 1: a campaign\'s success living only in one person\'s head is treated as a real risk, not a minor inconvenience, once that person is no longer the one watching it. A mistake to avoid: treating handover documentation as a formality to complete after a transition has already happened — the source frames it as a required step before a transition is considered complete.',
    4, 'What We Lost and Why, Rule H-2 (mandatory handover) and Section 8 (cost of the gap)'),
  lesson('cppc-m8-l4', 'cppc-m8', 4, 'Recurring Review: Daily Routines and Weekly Queries',
    'Beyond the automatic kill gates, the sources describe a lighter-touch recurring review discipline: a short daily routine covering the most important checks across active campaigns, and a set of standing weekly queries designed specifically to surface "leakage" — spend that is not being caught by any of the sharper automatic gates but is still quietly underperforming. The distinction matters: kill gates catch severe, unambiguous problems immediately, while the daily routine and weekly queries are the mechanism for catching the subtler, slower-building issues that a hard automatic rule is not well-suited to detect on its own. A mistake to avoid: assuming the kill gates alone are sufficient oversight — the sources pair them with a deliberate, recurring human review specifically because some problems do not trip an automatic gate at all.',
    4, 'Google PPC Strategy — 17 Months of Live Data, daily routine and weekly leakage-check sections'),

  // Module 9 — Google Ads Campaign Setup & Optimization
  lesson('cppc-m9-l1', 'cppc-m9', 1, 'The Performance Max Campaign Build Sequence',
    'The Google Ads setup source describes a defined build sequence for a Performance Max campaign: confirming the product feed is connected and healthy, setting an initial budget, naming the campaign following a consistent convention, configuring the ad assets, choosing a bidding strategy, and — importantly — starting the campaign without an aggressive ROAS target set from day one, since the algorithm needs an initial learning period to gather its own data before being asked to hit a specific efficiency goal. Skipping ahead to set a demanding target immediately is described as counterproductive, since it can constrain the volume of traffic the campaign is allowed to pursue while it is still learning. A mistake to avoid: setting an ambitious ROAS target on a brand-new campaign to "start strong" — the source describes this as working against the algorithm\'s own learning process, not accelerating it.',
    5, 'Google Ads Performance Max — Campaign Setup & Optimization Strategy, campaign-build and bidding-phase sections'),
  lesson('cppc-m9-l2', 'cppc-m9', 2, 'The Product-Feed Quality Gate',
    'Before a Performance Max campaign is expected to perform well, the source requires the underlying product feed to clear a specific quality bar: titles and descriptions within defined length limits, a correctly assigned product type, distinct listings for different colour or size variations rather than one listing standing in for several, adequately sized images, and pricing that matches what is actually charged at checkout — a mismatch here is treated as an automatic rejection risk, not a minor inconsistency. This gate exists because Performance Max relies heavily on the feed to understand what it is advertising; a weak or inconsistent feed undermines the campaign\'s targeting regardless of how well the budget and bidding are configured elsewhere. A mistake to avoid: treating feed quality as a one-time setup task — the source ties feed health directly to ongoing campaign troubleshooting, not just initial setup.',
    4, 'Google Ads Performance Max — Campaign Setup & Optimization Strategy, feed-requirements section'),
  lesson('cppc-m9-l3', 'cppc-m9', 3, 'Negative-Keyword Conditions That Stop Wasted Spend',
    'The source describes three specific, unambiguous conditions under which a search term should be added as a negative immediately, rather than left to accumulate more wasted spend: a meaningful number of clicks with zero conversions, spend on a single term reaching a defined share of the product\'s own price with no resulting sale, and a search term that is simply unrelated to what is actually being sold. Each condition is framed as an immediate action, not something to batch up and review later — the logic mirrors the kill-gate pattern from the previous module, applied here at the individual search-term level rather than the whole-campaign level. A mistake to avoid: waiting for a scheduled weekly review to add an obviously wasteful search term as a negative — the source treats all three conditions as reasons to act immediately, not at the next convenient checkpoint.',
    4, 'Google Ads Performance Max — Campaign Setup & Optimization Strategy, negative-keyword rules'),
  lesson('cppc-m9-l4', 'cppc-m9', 4, 'Staged Budget Review Once There Is Enough Data',
    'Once a campaign has accumulated enough performance history to judge fairly, the source describes a staged monthly budget review: strong ROAS clearly above a healthy bar supports a further budget increase; ROAS in a solid middle range supports holding the budget steady; and weaker ROAS triggers a specific fallback sequence — checking whether a short-term dip is masked by a longer trailing view, checking a 60-day view for a fuller picture, checking whether the trend is already improving, and only reducing budget once all of those checks still point to genuine underperformance. This staged fallback matters because it prevents a single weak stretch, viewed too narrowly, from triggering a budget cut that a slightly wider view would have shown was unnecessary. A mistake to avoid: reducing a campaign\'s budget based on one weak monthly figure without first working through the source\'s staged fallback checks — the sequence exists specifically to avoid an overreaction to a narrow snapshot.',
    3, 'Google Ads Performance Max — Campaign Setup & Optimization Strategy, monthly budget-review sequence'),

  // Module 10 — Meta Campaign Proposal Discipline
  lesson('cppc-m10-l1', 'cppc-m10', 1, 'Separating Cold and Mid-Funnel Audiences',
    'The Meta campaign proposal in this programme separates two audience strategies clearly: a cold-prospecting audience built broadly, without narrowing by specific interests, aimed at people who have had no prior contact with the brand; and a mid-funnel audience built from people who have already shown some engagement — for example, watching a meaningful share of a video, or engaging with the brand\'s page or profile — within a defined recent window. The proposal is explicit that people who have already visited the website or added something to a cart are deliberately excluded from both of these audiences, because that warmer group is reserved for a separate retargeting campaign rather than being folded into cold or mid-funnel targeting. This separation exists so that each campaign\'s budget is spent on the audience it is actually designed to reach, rather than overlapping with — and diluting — a different campaign\'s intended audience. A mistake to avoid: including website visitors or cart-adders in a cold or mid-funnel audience "to widen reach" — the proposal treats that overlap as a targeting mistake, not a bonus.',
    5, 'FB Ads New Campaign Proposal, audience-definition sections (Cold Prospecting, Mid-Funnel, exclusions)'),
  lesson('cppc-m10-l2', 'cppc-m10', 2, 'Budget Allocation and Success Conditions',
    'The proposal allocates a defined share of the overall daily budget to each new campaign — a larger share to the cold-prospecting campaign, reflecting its role in finding new customers, and a smaller share to the mid-funnel campaign, reflecting its narrower, already-warmer audience. Each campaign is also given an explicit early success condition, combining a click-through-rate bar with a minimum number of purchases within a short initial window, used to judge quickly whether the campaign is worth continuing to fund at its current settings. Defining success criteria before launch, rather than deciding afterward whether the results "feel" successful, is the same evidence-first discipline already seen throughout this programme, applied here specifically to a new campaign proposal. A mistake to avoid: launching a proposed campaign without first agreeing what result would count as success — the proposal treats that definition as part of the plan, not an afterthought once results start coming in.',
    5, 'FB Ads New Campaign Proposal, budget-allocation and success-condition sections'),
  lesson('cppc-m10-l3', 'cppc-m10', 3, 'The Manager-Approval Gate Before Launch',
    'This proposal is the clearest example in the entire Centralized PPC source set of an explicit "not yet approved" status: its own header states directly that manager approval is required before either campaign can launch, regardless of how thoroughly the audience, budget, and success criteria have been worked out. This is a useful anchor for the recommendation-versus-approval principle introduced in Module 1 — a proposal can be complete, well-reasoned, and ready to execute, and still not be an approved campaign until a named approval step has actually happened. The source also references an existing internal evaluation framework — informally described as requiring a pattern to repeat across independent creatives and to hold up without a discount attached — without fully defining it within this one document; this programme does not invent the missing detail and treats that framework as referenced but not fully specified here. A mistake to avoid: treating a well-prepared proposal as functionally equivalent to an approved campaign — this source is explicit that it is not, until approval is actually given.',
    4, 'FB Ads New Campaign Proposal, header approval requirement (referenced evaluation framework not fully defined in this source)'),
  lesson('cppc-m10-l4', 'cppc-m10', 4, 'Why This Track Has One Module, Not Several',
    'The Centralized PPC source set includes only a single Meta document, and this programme deliberately does not stretch that one source into several thinner modules to make the programme look more balanced across platforms. Instead, this single module covers everything the source actually supports — audience strategy, budget allocation, success criteria, and the approval gate — at a depth matched to what is genuinely there. This is a direct application of the module-count principle used throughout this programme: module count follows the evidence available in the sources, not an even split across platforms for its own sake. A mistake to avoid: assuming a shorter track reflects Meta being less important to Centralized PPC\'s work — it reflects only how much source material was available to build training content from, nothing more.',
    2, 'Programme-wide module-count principle — see Programme Sources'),

  // Module 11 — eBay Campaign Setup & Listing Readiness
  lesson('cppc-m11-l1', 'cppc-m11', 1, 'The Promoted Listings Setup Path',
    'The eBay sources describe two ways into Promoted Listings advertising: an Advanced (Pay-Per-Click) route, set up through Seller Hub\'s Marketing tools by creating a campaign and organising it into ad groups, and a Smart Campaign route, set up through a separate Advertising Dashboard, which automates more of the targeting decision-making on the seller\'s behalf. Choosing between them is a setup decision made once per campaign, not something switched back and forth casually, since the two routes structure the underlying campaign differently. This mirrors the Amazon track\'s distinction between automatic and manual targeting — both platforms offer a more hands-off, automated route alongside a more hands-on, structured one, and the sources treat the choice as meaningful rather than cosmetic. A mistake to avoid: assuming the two setup routes produce an identical underlying campaign structure — the sources describe them as genuinely different paths, not two names for the same thing.',
    5, 'eBay Ads BGCT, campaign-creation sections (Promoted Listings Advanced, Smart Campaign)'),
  lesson('cppc-m11-l2', 'cppc-m11', 2, 'The Minimum-Price Eligibility Gate',
    'Before a listing can be promoted at all, the sources apply a minimum selling-price gate — a listing priced below that floor is not eligible for promotion, with a lower exception carved out for a small set of named product categories. This is conceptually the same idea as the Amazon eligibility gates from Module 2: a product below a certain price point is judged not to generate enough margin to justify the cost of advertising it, so it is excluded from paid promotion entirely rather than promoted inefficiently. This programme reports this concept as a genuine gate the sources apply, without treating it as connected to any of the nine excluded conflicts — it is independently corroborated across more than one eBay source, which is part of why it is taught here with confidence. A mistake to avoid: assuming every listing on the account is automatically eligible for Promoted Listings — the sources apply a specific price floor before a listing is considered a candidate at all.',
    4, 'eBay Ads BGCT, pricing-rule section; eBay Team Guides, promotions eligibility section'),
  lesson('cppc-m11-l3', 'cppc-m11', 3, 'Listing-Quality Inputs That Support a Campaign',
    'The sources describe a set of listing-quality inputs that support a healthy Promoted Listings campaign once it launches: a clear, appropriately detailed title within a defined length range, enough product images to let a shopper evaluate the item properly, and pricing that stays broadly competitive with what similar listings are charging. These inputs work the same way the Amazon listing-quality gate does in Module 2 — a well-targeted campaign spending against a weak listing still tends to underperform, because the traffic it buys has a harder time converting once it arrives. A mistake to avoid: treating campaign setup and listing quality as two unrelated workstreams — the sources present listing readiness as a direct input to how well the resulting campaign can be expected to perform.',
    4, 'eBay Ads BGCT, Listing Quality Score section'),
  lesson('cppc-m11-l4', 'cppc-m11', 4, 'Keyword Review Cadence',
    'The sources describe a recurring weekly review of a campaign\'s keyword performance, specifically looking for terms with a very low click-through rate or no sales at all over the review period, and removing a meaningful share of the weakest-performing terms at each pass rather than leaving them to keep drawing spend indefinitely. This connects to the negative-keyword logic already taught in the Google Ads track (Module 9) — both platforms treat a demonstrably unproductive keyword as something to actively remove on a regular cadence, not something to leave running out of inertia. A mistake to avoid: reviewing keyword performance only when a campaign is already visibly underperforming overall — the sources describe this as a standing weekly habit, applied whether or not the campaign looks like it currently has a problem.',
    3, 'eBay Ads BGCT, keyword review cadence section'),

  // Module 12 — eBay Monitoring, Optimization & Escalation
  lesson('cppc-m12-l1', 'cppc-m12', 1, 'Daily and Weekly Review Cadence',
    'The sources describe a standing review rhythm for an active eBay advertising account: every active PPC campaign is checked daily, while search-term performance is reviewed on a weekly cycle, and a broader account-health check (covering account performance and defect rate more generally) also runs daily. This layered cadence — some things checked every day, others weekly — mirrors the pattern already seen in the Google Ads track\'s daily routine and weekly leakage-check queries: a fast, lightweight check catches urgent problems quickly, while a slower, more thorough check catches the subtler issues a daily glance would miss. A mistake to avoid: assuming a single weekly check is sufficient oversight for an active campaign — the sources describe daily campaign checks as a separate, additional requirement, not something the weekly review replaces.',
    5, 'eBay Team Guides, Account Health and Advanced PPC Management sections'),
  lesson('cppc-m12-l2', 'cppc-m12', 2, 'ACoS Escalation to the Leader Role',
    'The sources describe an ACoS ceiling above which a campaign requires explicit approval from the Leader role before it can continue running unchanged, and a separate, higher ACoS level at which the source calls for immediate corrective action regardless of approval status. This is a distinct, non-conflicting figure from the disputed advertising-rate range excluded from this programme (see Module 14) — the ACoS approval ceiling and the specific starting advertising-rate percentage are two different rules in the sources, and only the second one is excluded here. The pattern itself — a defined threshold that triggers mandatory approval before continuing, and a higher one that triggers immediate action — is the same escalation shape already seen in the Amazon and Google Ads tracks. A mistake to avoid: confusing the ACoS approval ceiling with the excluded advertising-rate figure — this programme teaches the former as a genuine, source-backed rule and deliberately excludes the latter.',
    5, 'eBay Team Guides, Priority PPC Targets section (ACoS approval ceiling only — excludes the disputed advertising-rate range; see Programme Sources)'),
  lesson('cppc-m12-l3', 'cppc-m12', 3, 'Pausing After a Sustained Period With No Sales',
    'One eBay source describes pausing a listing\'s advertising after it has run for a defined number of consecutive days with spend but no resulting sales, following a pause-analyse-fix-retry sequence rather than simply switching ads back on unchanged once the underlying issue (commonly a pricing, image, or offer problem) has been addressed. This is the same underlying idea as the Amazon zero-order pause logic from Module 5 and the Google Ads kill-gate logic from Module 8, applied here at the eBay listing level: spend without any resulting sale, sustained long enough to rule out ordinary day-to-day variation, is treated as a signal to stop and investigate rather than keep spending. This programme teaches the general "sustained no-sales period leads to a pause-and-fix response" concept without asserting the exact number of days as a confirmed universal figure, since it comes from a source whose other guidance conflicts with a sibling eBay document on a related point (see Module 14). A mistake to avoid: reactivating a paused, no-sales listing without first addressing whatever underlying issue caused the lack of sales — the sources describe a fix step as a required part of the sequence, not an optional one.',
    4, 'Rules of Successful Ad Strategy — The Accelerator Method, dead-listing and no-sales pause sections (non-conflicting portion only)'),
  lesson('cppc-m12-l4', 'cppc-m12', 4, 'The Weekly PPC-Manager Report',
    'The sources assign a specific, recurring reporting duty to a "PPC manager" role: a weekly report covering which campaigns are spending the most, which are running the highest ACoS, what actions were taken in response, and what result is expected from those actions. This closes the loop on the monitoring and escalation ideas taught earlier in this module — a threshold being crossed is only half the picture; the sources also expect a standing record of what was actually done about it and what outcome is expected, reviewed on a fixed weekly cycle rather than left informal. A mistake to avoid: treating the weekly report as a summary written after the fact with no real effect — the source frames it as the mechanism that makes the account\'s escalation and action-taking visible and accountable on a predictable schedule.',
    2, 'eBay Team Guides, weekly PPC manager reporting requirement'),

  // Module 13 — Cross-Platform Reporting & Evidence Standards
  lesson('cppc-m13-l1', 'cppc-m13', 1, 'Reporting Cadence Patterns Across Platforms',
    'Every platform in this programme describes some form of recurring report, but the exact cadence differs by platform and, in Amazon\'s case, even differs between two sibling sources describing what should be the same reporting rhythm — this programme does not resolve that specific disagreement and does not assert a single, universal reporting cadence that applies everywhere. What is consistent across every platform, and what this lesson teaches with confidence, is the underlying pattern: some form of regular, scheduled reporting exists on every platform this programme covers, it flows to a specific role rather than sitting unread, and it is expected to happen whether or not anything unusual occurred that period. A mistake to avoid: assuming a single fixed reporting schedule applies identically across every platform — the sources support the pattern of recurring reporting, not one universal timetable.',
    5, 'Amazon PPC Campaign Management BGCT Standard Handbook, reporting section; eBay Team Guides, weekly report section (exact Amazon cadence excluded — see Programme Sources)'),
  lesson('cppc-m13-l2', 'cppc-m13', 2, 'A Shared Evidence and Audit-Trail Discipline',
    'Across Amazon, Google, and eBay, the sources describe the same underlying evidence expectation using different specific mechanisms — an Amazon audit log recording who changed a rule and when, a Google Ads handover document capturing why a campaign works and what must not be changed carelessly, and an eBay weekly report naming specific actions taken and their expected results. In every case, the shared principle from Module 1 holds: a change, a decision, or an instruction that exists only in someone\'s memory or in an informal conversation is treated as if it did not happen, regardless of which platform it relates to. A mistake to avoid: assuming evidence requirements are a compliance formality specific to one platform — every platform in this programme applies some version of the same underlying discipline.',
    4, 'Bid Optimization Rule Configurator, audit-log requirement; What We Lost and Why, handover rule; eBay Team Guides, weekly report requirement'),
  lesson('cppc-m13-l3', 'cppc-m13', 3, 'Recommendation Versus Approval, Restated With Platform Examples',
    'Module 1 introduced the distinction between a system-generated recommendation and an approved decision; every platform track in this programme has since reinforced it with its own concrete example — Amazon\'s rule-engine outputs sitting alongside named roles that can create, edit, or disable a rule; Google Ads\' budget-ceiling and protected-campaign rules requiring approval before a strong campaign is changed; eBay\'s ACoS ceiling requiring Leader approval before a high-spend campaign continues unchanged; and Meta\'s proposal requiring explicit manager sign-off before any launch at all. Seeing the same underlying principle expressed four different ways across four different platforms is itself useful evidence that this is not a one-off rule specific to a single source, but a consistent expectation running through how Centralized PPC is meant to operate everywhere. A mistake to avoid: treating this principle as an Amazon-specific or a Meta-specific rule — every platform track in this programme applies its own version of it.',
    4, 'Programme-wide synthesis — see Modules 3, 8, 10, and 12'),
  lesson('cppc-m13-l4', 'cppc-m13', 4, 'Handover as a Cross-Platform Safeguard',
    'The Google Ads handover requirement from Module 8 is the most fully documented example in this programme, but the underlying need it addresses — protecting a campaign\'s context and rationale when the person managing it changes — is not unique to Google Ads. Amazon\'s Document Control approach (a named owner, validator, and approver on record for its governing handbook) and eBay\'s weekly reporting duty both serve a related purpose: making sure a campaign\'s current state and reasoning do not live only in one person\'s head. This programme treats handover discipline as a cross-platform principle worth applying wherever a campaign or account changes hands, even on platforms where the sources describe it less explicitly than Google Ads does. A mistake to avoid: assuming handover discipline is only formally required where a source spells it out in detail — the underlying risk it protects against exists on every platform, whether or not each source describes the safeguard with the same level of detail.',
    3, 'What We Lost and Why, Rule H-2; Amazon PPC Campaign Management BGCT Standard Handbook, Document Control section'),

  // Module 14 — Roles, Approval Gates & Programme Closure
  lesson('cppc-m14-l1', 'cppc-m14', 1, 'A Generic Role Model Across Platforms',
    'Rather than inventing a single unified role structure the sources do not actually describe, this programme keeps each platform\'s roles as its own sources define them, generalised only to a role title: on Amazon, a Lead-level role with full rule-management rights, a Team Leader-level role that can create and edit but not delete or disable rules, and a Staff-level role limited to read-only access; on Google Ads, a Team Lead role that approves larger budget changes and protected-campaign edits; on Meta, an unnamed Manager role that must approve a campaign before it launches; and on eBay, a Leader role that approves high-ACoS campaigns and a separate PPC-manager role that owns the weekly report. This programme never assumes one of these roles is the same person or the same authority level as another platform\'s role just because the titles sound similar — the sources describe them separately, and this programme keeps them separate. A mistake to avoid: assuming an approval authority confirmed on one platform automatically extends to a different platform — each platform\'s approval role is scoped to that platform\'s own sources.',
    4, 'Bid Optimization Rule Configurator, role table; What We Lost and Why; FB Ads New Campaign Proposal; eBay Team Guides'),
  lesson('cppc-m14-l2', 'cppc-m14', 2, 'When Human Approval Is Required Versus When a Recommendation Is Enough',
    'Pulling together every approval gate covered across this programme, a consistent boundary emerges: routine, well-understood, incremental adjustments within an already-established pattern (a small bid nudge, a modest budget top-up within the rules already taught) are generally handled by the automated rule logic itself, while anything larger, riskier, or outside that established pattern — a large budget increase, a change to a protected or high-ACoS campaign, or launching a brand-new campaign at all — consistently requires an explicit human approval step first. This is the practical, operational version of the recommendation-versus-approval principle from Module 1: the size and risk of a change is what determines whether a recommendation is sufficient on its own or whether it must wait for approval. A mistake to avoid: assuming "the system already flagged it" is the same as "a person has approved it" — this programme has shown, on every platform, that these are treated as two separate steps.',
    4, 'Programme-wide synthesis — see Modules 1, 4, 8, 10, and 12'),
  lesson('cppc-m14-l3', 'cppc-m14', 3, 'Why Nine Specific Items Are Excluded — A Recap',
    'This programme has, at different points, deliberately avoided stating a specific number for nine topics: the Amazon SP minimum star rating, the Amazon budget-review cadence, the Amazon ASINs-per-ad-group rule, the Amazon UK-versus-DE/FR/IT high-ACOS pause threshold, the un-localised DE/FR/IT currency figures, the Amazon month-boundary split date, the Amazon fast-moving-product definition, the eBay advertising-rate range, and the Amazon budget-tier structure. In every one of these nine cases, two or more source documents that should agree with each other on the same rule instead state different, conflicting values — and rather than picking one value, averaging them, or guessing which is more likely correct, this programme leaves all nine out of every lesson, every Skill Check question, and the final practical task. The full register, including exactly which source documents disagree and how, is recorded in this programme\'s documentation rather than repeated here. A mistake to avoid: assuming a number recalled from outside this programme fills one of these nine gaps correctly — none of these nine topics has a single, agreed source-backed value to recall in the first place.',
    3, 'Full exclusions register — see docs/centralized-ppc-team-exclusions.md'),
  lesson('cppc-m14-l4', 'cppc-m14', 4, 'Preparing for the Final Practical Task',
    'This programme has covered a shared PPC foundation, six Amazon modules spanning campaign structure through multi-market operations, two Google Ads modules, one Meta module, two eBay modules, and two cross-platform reporting and governance modules — throughout, preserving the sources\' exact terminology, formulas, and sequencing wherever they agree, and deliberately excluding nine specific items wherever they do not. The final practical task asks you to apply this across a short, fictional, cross-platform PPC planning and review exercise. It is entirely PROTOTYPE_ONLY: it does not connect to any real advertising platform, cannot change a live campaign or spend real budget, is not an approval or certification of any kind, and does not require anyone\'s sign-off. Completing it is optional and has no effect on programme completion, which is reached once every required lesson is complete and every module Skill Check has been passed.',
    3, 'Programme-wide synthesis; see the Final Practical Task screen for details'),
];
